{"kind":"report","text":"CLOSE cb3debcf: the successor-staleness regression at 6da91f70 should not be revived. It is correct, it still applies, and it guards nothing the suite does not already guard. But the mutation work found something in main that nothing guards at all, and that is worth more than the 49 lines.\n\nWHAT THE TEST IS. TestMergeSuccessorIsNotStaleThroughTheCandidatesItsMergeMustRetire pins a real incident: at merge e677dd8f the successor's causal chain ran successor to receipt to approval to candidate, retirement is retroactive, so every successor came out stale at birth, gs merge found no live non-stale successor at any published path, and it exited after HEAD had already moved. Resuming failed identically. That is a merge that could neither finish nor be retried, and pinning it end to end is a reasonable thing to want.\n\nIT STILL WORKS. Extracted onto current main e72ccaff it compiles and passes. Note that reviving means lifting the function, not taking the file: the branch's fold_test.go is 856 lines behind main, so a checkout would delete most of the package's tests.\n\nIT IS NOT THE SOLE GUARD FOR ANYTHING. Your condition was that it must fail when the behaviour it pins is broken. I mutated the two fold rules it depends on, singly, and ran the whole internal/workroom suite each time. Neutering the direct-basis merge-plan exemption fails TestMergeSuccessorRestingOnThePredecessorItRetiresStaysCurrent and TestMergePlanIsNotLentToARecordTheMergeDidNotPublish -- and does NOT fail the revived test. Neutering the transitive cover fails nothing anywhere. So under one mutation the existing tests catch it first, and under the other nothing catches it including this one. It adds a multi-path axis its single-path twin lacks, but no defect reaches it that the suite misses.\n\nTHE FINDING, WHICH IS THE REASON THIS LANE WAS WORTH RUNNING. fold.go:1385, the transitive merge-plan staleness cover -- if plan is non-nil and the basis is stale and stalenessCoveredByMergePlan says the plan accounts for it, skip. I replaced that condition with a constant false, so the cover never applies. go vet clean, internal/workroom passes in 6.954s, cmd/gs passes in 121.360s. The entire suite is green with a live fold rule disabled. Nothing in the tree distinguishes that rule from its absence.\n\nI am not claiming it is dead code. It may be unreachable under the fixtures the tests build while mattering in production, which is the more likely and more uncomfortable reading, since the sibling rule at the direct-basis arm is well covered. Either way the current state is that a merge-succession staleness rule can be removed without any gate objecting, and that is a hole in exactly the machinery this dormant branch was written about.\n\nThat deserves its own request rather than a line in a closure report, and it is yours to assign, the way you took the residual gap out of my temporal-staleness closure. I have not filed one.\n\nI have not deleted the branch ref, per your assert ad92eac2: this report cites line numbers inside 6da91f70 and deleting the ref is what eventually makes them unfetchable."}